\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Proves that positive uniform near-zero concentration on a compact time interval implies an atom at a positive time under narrow continuity.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{When uniform concentration already forces an atom.} Suppose $t\mapsto F_t$ is a narrowly continuous family of finite positive measures on $[0,\infty)$ for $0\leq t\leq t_0$, with $F_0(\{0\})=0$. Assume the source's weaker quantity is positive. Then some $c>0$, sequence $\epsilon_j\downarrow0$ and times $t_j\in[0,t_0]$ satisfy $F_{t_j}([0,\epsilon_j])\geq c$. Extract a subsequence with $t_j\to t_*$. For any $\delta>0$, choose a continuous compactly supported cutoff $\chi_\delta$ equal to one on $[0,\delta/2]$, between zero and one, and supported in $[0,\delta]$. For large $j$,
\[
 \int\chi_\delta\,dF_{t_j}\geq c,
 \quad\text{hence}\quad F_{t_*}([0,\delta])\geq c.
\]
The limit uses time continuity. Taking $\delta\downarrow0$ and continuity from above of the finite measure gives $F_{t_*}(\{0\})\geq c$. Since $F_0$ has no atom, $t_*>0$.

\paragraph{Strength of the extra hypothesis.} Only continuity against these cutoffs is needed; smooth $C_b^2$ cutoffs can be used. But it must hold on the whole compact interval, including any candidate condensation time. Without time compactness or continuity, narrow spikes may concentrate along times that do not yield an admissible limiting state. The proof does not establish the positive lower bound $c$ from the collision equation.

\paragraph{Original target.} This is a conditional reduction from the weaker source alternative to a finite-time atom, retaining absolutely continuous initial data and the temporal supremum. Producing a solution from suitable noncondensed low-temperature data with the required concentration estimate is still the first gap. The source is existential, not a demand for condensation from every datum, and no such stronger assertion is used.

